## In 30 seconds

The **component** module ships layout Models: **StackModel** (fading page stack), **TabbedPaneModel**, **DockPaneModel**, **WorkbenchModel**. Configure with fluent `*Config` beans and navigate via waves (`StackWaves`, `TabbedPaneModel.ADD`, etc.).

Quick Start
=========================

Add the component module and attach a `StackModel` with a `PageEnum` for animated page navigation.

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af</groupId>
    <artifactId>component</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

**Minimal example — stack with enum-driven pages (FXML showcase pattern):**

```java
@Override
protected void initModel() {
    InnerComponent<StackModel> stack = CBuilder.innerComponent(StackModel.class, FXMLPage.class);
    this.stackModel = findInnerComponent(stack);
}

@Override
protected void showView() {
    view().node().setCenter(this.stackModel.node());
}

// Navigate to another page:
sendWave(WBuilder.wave()
    .waveType(StackWaves.SHOW_PAGE_ENUM)
    .addDatas(WBuilder.waveData(StackWaves.PAGE_ENUM, FXMLPage.HybridFxml)));
```

`StackModel` uses `ShowFadingModelCommand` internally — page switches fade automatically.


Component Library Overview
=================================

The `component` module provides reusable, configurable UI container models that cover common application layout patterns:

* **StackModel** — Page-based navigation with animated transitions.
* **TabbedPaneModel** — Tabbed container with drag-and-drop tab reordering.
* **DockPaneModel** — Dockable panel layout with configurable orientation.
* **WorkbenchModel** — IDE-style workbench combining dock panes and tabs.

Each container is configured via a dedicated configuration bean and communicates through JRebirth waves.


StackModel
=========================

`StackModel` manages a `StackPane` that shows one child Model at a time. It uses `ShowFadingModelCommand` for animated transitions between pages.


```java
@Override
protected void initModel() {
    listen(new DefaultWaveChecker<>(StackWaves.STACK_NAME, getStackName()), StackWaves.SHOW_PAGE_MODEL);
    listen(new ClassWaveChecker<>(StackWaves.PAGE_ENUM, getPageEnumClass()), StackWaves.SHOW_PAGE_ENUM);
}

public void doShowPageEnum(final PageEnum pageEnum, final Wave wave) {
    if (getPageEnumClass() != null && getPageEnumClass().equals(pageEnum.getClass())) {
        showPage(pageEnum.getModelKey(), wave);
    }
}

private void showPage(final UniqueKey<? extends Model> pageModelKey, final Wave wave) {
    if (pageModelKey != null && !pageModelKey.equals(this.currentModelKey)) {
        final DisplayModelWaveBean waveBean = DisplayModelWaveBean.create()
            .childrenPlaceHolder(view().node().getChildren())
            .appendChild(false)
            .showModelKey(pageModelKey)
            .hideModelKey(this.currentModelKey);
        this.currentModelKey = waveBean.showModelKey();
        sendWave(WBuilder.callCommand(ShowFadingModelCommand.class).waveBean(waveBean).relatedWave(wave));
    }
}
```

`showPage` builds the wave bean with `appendChild(false)` and fires `ShowFadingModelCommand` — this is where cross-fade transitions run.

### PageEnum

Pages are mapped via a `PageEnum` that associates enum constants with Model keys:

```java
public enum MyPages implements PageEnum {
    Home, Settings, Profile;

    @Override
    public UniqueKey<? extends Model> getModelKey() {
        switch (this) {
            case Home:     return Key.create(HomeModel.class);
            case Settings: return Key.create(SettingsModel.class);
            case Profile:  return Key.create(ProfileModel.class);
            default:       return Key.create(HomeModel.class);
        }
    }
}
```

### StackWaves

Navigation is triggered by sending waves defined in `StackWaves`:


```java
public interface StackWaves {

    /*****************************************************************************************************/
    /** _________________________________________Wave Items.____________________________________________ */
    /*****************************************************************************************************/

    /** The name of the stack concerned. */
    WaveItemBase<String> STACK_NAME = new WaveItemBase<String>() {
    };

    /** The Enum class that list all pages displayable into the stack. */
    // WaveItem<Class<? extends PageEnum>> STACK_PAGES = new WaveItem<Class<? extends PageEnum>>() {
    // };

    /** The page to display (model class descriptor). */
    WaveItemBase<UniqueKey<? extends Model>> PAGE_MODEL_KEY = new WaveItemBase<UniqueKey<? extends Model>>() {
    };

    /** The page to display (enum descriptor). */
    WaveItemBase<PageEnum> PAGE_ENUM = new WaveItemBase<PageEnum>() {
    };

    /*****************************************************************************************************/
    /** _________________________________________Wave Types.____________________________________________ */
    /*****************************************************************************************************/

    /** Show Page (with Model) action. */
    WaveType SHOW_PAGE_MODEL = WBuilder.waveType("SHOW_PAGE_MODEL").items(PAGE_MODEL_KEY, STACK_NAME);

    /** Show Page (with Enum) action. */
    WaveType SHOW_PAGE_ENUM = WBuilder.waveType("SHOW_PAGE_ENUM").items(PAGE_ENUM/* , STACK_PAGES */);

}
```

Available wave types:

| WaveType | Parameters | Description |
|----------|-----------|-------------|
| `SHOW_PAGE_MODEL` | `PAGE_MODEL_KEY`, `STACK_NAME` | Show a page by its model key |
| `SHOW_PAGE_ENUM` | `PAGE_ENUM` | Show a page by its enum constant |

### Using StackModel

To create and configure a `StackModel`:

```java
InnerComponent<StackModel> stack = CBuilder.innerComponent(StackModel.class, MyPages.class);
StackModel stackModel = findInnerComponent(stack);
```

To navigate programmatically:

```java
sendWave(WBuilder.wave()
    .waveType(StackWaves.SHOW_PAGE_ENUM)
    .addDatas(WBuilder.waveData(StackWaves.PAGE_ENUM, MyPages.Settings)));
```

The first enum constant is automatically shown when the stack is first displayed.

### Configuration

`StackConfig` allows you to set:

* `stackName()` — Unique identifier for the stack (enables multiple stacks in the same app).
* `pageEnumClass()` — The `PageEnum` class that maps page IDs to model keys.


TabbedPaneModel
=========================

`TabbedPaneModel` provides a tabbed container with support for drag-and-drop tab reordering and animated tab insertion/removal.


```java
public static WaveType ADD = WBuilder.waveType("ADD_TAB").items(TAB);
public static WaveType REMOVE = WBuilder.waveType("REMOVE_TAB").items(TAB);

@Override
protected void initModel() {
    final WaveChecker waveChecker = wave ->
        ObjectUtility.equalsOrBothNull(wave.get(TAB_KEY), object().id());
    listen(waveChecker, ADD);
    listen(waveChecker, REMOVE);
}

@Override
protected void bind() {
    object().tabs().addListener(this::onTabsChanged);
}
```

Tab insertions and removals run through `SequentialTransition` on the JAT. Filter waves with `TabbedPaneModel.TAB_KEY` matching `TabbedPaneConfig.id()`.


### Adding Tabs

Tabs are added via waves or programmatically using `Dockable` instances:

```java
Dockable tab = Dockable.create()
    .modelKey(Key.create(MyContentModel.class))
    .name("My Tab");

sendWave(WBuilder.wave()
    .waveType(TabbedPaneModel.ADD)
    .addDatas(
        WBuilder.waveData(TabbedPaneModel.TAB, tab),
        WBuilder.waveData(TabbedPaneModel.TAB_KEY, "myTabbedPane")));
```

### Configuration

`TabbedPaneConfig` allows you to set:

* `id()` — Unique identifier for this tabbed pane.
* `orientation()` — Tab bar position: `TabbedPaneOrientation.top`, `bottom`, `left`, or `right`.
* `tabs()` — Initial list of `Dockable` tabs.
* `styleClass()` — CSS style class applied to the container.


DockPaneModel
=========================

`DockPaneModel` provides a dockable panel layout that splits space between child panes. Child panes can be other `DockPaneModel` instances (for nesting) or `TabbedPaneModel` instances.


```java
public static WaveType ADD = WBuilder.waveType("ADD_PART").items(MODEL);

@Override
protected void initModel() {
    listen(wave -> ObjectUtility.equalsOrBothNull(wave.get(DOCK_KEY), object().id()), ADD, REMOVE);
    for (final PartConfig<?, ?> pc : object().panes()) {
        callCommand(AddDockCommand.class,
            DockWaveBean.create().dockConfig(object()).parts(pc));
    }
}

private void onPanesChanged(final ListChangeListener.Change<? extends PartConfig<?, ?>> change) {
    while (change.next()) {
        if (change.wasRemoved()) {
            callCommand(RemoveDockCommand.class, DockWaveBean.create().dockConfig(object())
                .parts(change.getRemoved().toArray(new PartConfig[0])));
        }
        if (change.wasAdded()) {
            callCommand(AddDockCommand.class, DockWaveBean.create().dockConfig(object())
                .parts(change.getList().get(change.getFrom())));
        }
    }
}
```

Pane list changes dispatch `AddDockCommand` / `RemoveDockCommand`. Nest `DockPaneConfig` entries inside `panes()` for multi-level splits.


### Configuration

`DockPaneConfig` allows you to set:

* `id()` — Unique identifier for this dock pane.
* `orientation()` — `DockPaneOrientation.horizontal` or `vertical` (split direction).
* `panes()` — Child `PartConfig` instances (can be nested `DockPaneConfig` or `TabbedPaneConfig`).

### Adding/Removing Parts

Parts are managed via `AddDockCommand` / `RemoveDockCommand` or through the wave system:

```java
sendWave(WBuilder.wave()
    .waveType(DockPaneModel.ADD)
    .addDatas(
        WBuilder.waveData(DockPaneModel.MODEL, myModel),
        WBuilder.waveData(DockPaneModel.DOCK_KEY, "myDock")));
```


WorkbenchModel
=========================

`WorkbenchModel` provides an IDE-style workbench layout. It combines `DockPaneModel` and `TabbedPaneModel` into a configurable multi-panel workspace.


`WorkbenchModel` is a thin wrapper; real workbench layouts are composed from nested `DockPaneModel` and `TabbedPaneModel` instances. The workbench showcase `TabDemoModel` builds a 2×2 grid by nesting `DockPaneConfig` and `TabbedPaneConfig`:

```java
final DockPaneModel rootDock = getModel(DockPaneModel.class,
    DockPaneConfig.create()
        .id("RootDock")
        .orientation(DockPaneOrientation.vertical)
        .panes(topDockConfig, bottomDockConfig));
node().setCenter(rootDock.node());
```

Each quadrant wraps a `TabbedPaneConfig` with its own tab orientation (`top`, `right`, `bottom`, `left`). See the full listing below.


### Example: Building a Workbench

The showcase demonstrates how to compose a complex workbench layout using nested dock panes and tabbed panes:


```java
@Register(value = ModuleModel.class)
public class TabDemoModel extends DefaultSimpleModel<BorderPane> implements ModuleModel {

	private boolean done = false;

	@Override
	protected void initSimpleView() {
		super.initSimpleView();

		final String topLeft = "TopLeft";
		final String topRight = "TopRight";
		final String bottomLeft = "BottomLeft";
		final String bottomRight = "BottomRight";
		final DockPaneModel rootDock = getModel(DockPaneModel.class,
				DockPaneConfig.create()
						.id("RootDock")
						.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.vertical)
						.panes(
								DockPaneConfig.create()
										.id("TopDock")
										.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
										.panes(
												DockPaneConfig.create()
														.id(topLeft+"Dock")
														.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
														.panes(TabbedPaneConfig.create()
																.id(topLeft)
																.styleClass("Top Left")
																.orientation(TabbedPaneOrientation.top)
																.tabs(
																		dockable("1"),
																		dockable("2"),
																		dockable("3"),
																		dockable("4"),
																		dockable("5"),
																		dockable("6")
																		)),
												DockPaneConfig.create()
														.id(topRight+"Dock")
														.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
														.panes(TabbedPaneConfig.create()
																.id(topRight)
																.styleClass("Top Right")
																.orientation(TabbedPaneOrientation.right)
																.tabs(
																		dockable("7"),
																		dockable("8"),
																		dockable("9")
																		))),
								DockPaneConfig.create()
										.id("BottomDock")
										.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
										.panes(
												DockPaneConfig.create()
														.id(bottomLeft+"Dock")
														.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
														.panes(TabbedPaneConfig.create()
																.id(bottomLeft)
																.styleClass("Bottom Left")
																.orientation(TabbedPaneOrientation.bottom)
																.tabs(
																		dockable("15"),
																		dockable("16")
																		)),
												DockPaneConfig.create()
														.id(bottomRight+"Dock")
														.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
														.panes(TabbedPaneConfig.create()
																.id(bottomRight)
																.styleClass("Bottom Right")
																.orientation(TabbedPaneOrientation.left)
																.tabs(
																		dockable("10"),
																		dockable("11"),
																		dockable("12"),
																		dockable("13"),
																		dockable("14")
																		)
																))));
		node().setCenter(rootDock.node());
	}
}
```

This example creates a 2x2 grid layout:

* **Top-left**: Tabbed pane with tabs on top
* **Top-right**: Tabbed pane with tabs on the right
* **Bottom-left**: Tabbed pane with tabs on the bottom
* **Bottom-right**: Tabbed pane with tabs on the left

Each section is wrapped in nested `DockPaneConfig` instances to control the split directions.


Configuration Beans
=========================

All component containers use configuration beans that follow a fluent builder pattern:

### StackConfig

```java
StackConfig.create()
    .stackName("main")
    .pageEnumClass(MyPages.class)
```

### TabbedPaneConfig

```java
TabbedPaneConfig.create()
    .id("editor")
    .orientation(TabbedPaneOrientation.top)
    .styleClass("editor-tabs")
    .tabs(dockable1, dockable2)
```

### DockPaneConfig

```java
DockPaneConfig.create()
    .id("rootDock")
    .orientation(DockPaneOrientation.vertical)
    .panes(topConfig, bottomConfig)
```

### PartConfig

`PartConfig` is the common supertype for all pane configurations (`TabbedPaneConfig`, `DockPaneConfig`). It allows heterogeneous nesting of container types within a dock layout.


